# Lintian overrides for KiChat. Installed as /usr/share/lintian/overrides/kichat,
# which is where lintian reads them from once the package is installed.
#
# Only findings that are false positives or deliberate leave a line here. The
# hardening warnings are not overridden: -buildmode=pie and the external linker
# with -z,now in the Makefile are what clear those, because they were real
# findings about a real binary.
#
# The contexts below are wildcards on purpose. On lintian 2.117 the literal
# context it prints (".comment", "Assistent Assistant") does not match, and every
# literal spelling tried — the displayed context, the file path, the tag name
# included — was reported back as `mismatched-override`. `*` is the one form that
# suppresses, which was measured by repacking the same tree with each variant.
#
# `make lint-deb` checks the .deb twice: raw, then with the tags listed here
# suppressed. The residual list stays visible, so this file has to earn its lines.

# The spellings lintian flags are German UI strings and Go runtime symbols that
# happen to look like English misspellings: "Assistent" is the German for
# assistant, and "classs", "ment", "nAm" are substrings of identifiers
# (classString, alignment, nAm...) rather than words anyone wrote.
kichat binary: spelling-error-in-binary *

# gcc leaves a .comment section with the compiler version in the object files it
# produced for the cgo/GTK bindings, and Go carries it into the linked binary.
# There is no supported way to drop one input section from a Go link, and it is
# ~50 bytes of build provenance in a 22 MB binary.
kichat binary: binary-has-unneeded-section *
